# What Vercel needs to serve the download page: vercel.json (which points the
# build at apps/site) and apps/site itself. Nothing else.
#
# Without this, a deploy uploads the whole monorepo — 11.5 MB tracked, ~5.6 MB of
# it the pet spritesheets — and `vercel deploy` fails outright with "Request body
# too large. Limit: 10mb". It also keeps a git-connected deploy from shipping the
# desktop app's assets to a CDN that has no use for them.
#
# `installCommand` and `buildCommand` in vercel.json are no-ops, so none of the
# JS toolchain is needed either.
#
# CAREFUL: this file REPLACES Vercel's default ignores rather than adding to them.
# Omitting node_modules here made a deploy attempt try to upload 663 MB and burn
# the free upload quota ("more than 5000 requests, try again in 24 hours"). Every
# build/dependency directory has to be listed explicitly.

# Dependencies and build output — must be listed; nothing is ignored by default
node_modules
.git
target
dist
.turbo
.next
coverage
.build
.build-x86
playwright-report
test-results
apps/desktop/src-tauri/target
apps/desktop/src-tauri/gen
native/browser-host/target

# Workspaces that are not the site
apps/api
apps/desktop
apps/e2e
apps/web
apps/worker
packages
extensions
native
deploy

# Repo content with no bearing on a static page
docs
domain
scripts
*.md
docker-compose.yml
eslint.config.mjs
tsconfig.base.json
turbo.json
pnpm-lock.yaml
pnpm-workspace.yaml
